WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:18.410
Talking on the topics related
to social and demographic

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:23.180
research, they are Allan
Dafoe and Beth Noveck.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:25.700
Our first speaker
will be Allan Dafoe.

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:29.180
Assistant Professor of Political
Science at Yale University,

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:32.060
and faculty Fellow at both
the Institution for Social

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:33.900
and Policy Studies
and the MacMillan

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:37.010
Center for International
and Area Studies,

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:40.040
Professor Dafoe's research
examines the causes of war

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:43.850
and statistical methods for
credible, causal inference.

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:48.560
He won the Leamer-Rosenthal
Prize for Open Social Science

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:51.500
as an Emerging Researcher
for his work improving

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.450
transparency practices.

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:56.000
His PhD in Political Science
is from the University

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:57.740
of California, Berkeley.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:00.440
And he has an MA in
Economics from Berkeley,

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:03.230
and an undergraduate degree
from McMaster University.

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:06.378
So welcome, Professor Dafoe.

00:01:06.378 --> 00:01:08.858
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:30.520
Thanks so much.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:32.860
So it's a real pleasure
to have Brian just share

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:36.820
with us all the fantastic
tools that they're building.

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:38.920
And in many ways, much
of what I normally

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:40.470
would say to a
typical audience, I

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:42.670
am going to skip
through, because I

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:47.140
think both this audience is
more expert in these topics.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.690
And also, Brian covered
a lot of motivation

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:54.130
and overall philosophy
about transparency

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:56.660
that I would otherwise say.

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:59.900
So transparency is
crucial to science.

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:01.880
Science is a public enterprise.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:07.030
We need to make knowledge claims
that are then interrogated

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:08.500
in a public manner.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:09.669
But it's not just public.

00:02:09.669 --> 00:02:14.034
It requires that I
trust that the claims

00:02:14.034 --> 00:02:15.700
that the community
has accepted, or that

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:20.170
are in some level of regard
and esteem, are reliable.

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:25.360
The mechanisms of science have
to help me know what claims I

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:27.170
can rely on, and
which ones I can't.

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:32.180
Otherwise, I have to review
every argument that I

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:34.180
might want to cite or
method I want to build on.

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:36.160
And that's just not practical.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:39.760
But unfortunately, we
know from cases of fraud

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:44.620
and just neglect and
non-reproducible results,

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:47.560
and in particular, from
the systematic study

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:51.070
of 100 replications
in psychology,

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:57.160
that on the order of maybe 20%
to 50% of experimental results

00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:59.740
do not reproduce.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:04.580
In the sense that there
might be no result there,

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:05.440
it's actually zero.

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:09.130
And the original result was
a scientist finding noise,

00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:13.970
or the result is much weaker
than the initial publication.

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:17.800
OK, so we're trying
to build this edifice.

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:20.260
But it's only as good as the
materials that are coming in.

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:24.190
And we need a better process
for screening those materials.

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:27.700
A building that's built on
uneven terrain or soft ground

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:29.770
will not get very
high, similarly,

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:34.030
a building that has materials
with uneven quality.

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:38.360
So I'm going to rush
through two aspects of this.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:42.190
One is sharing data
and how we change

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:44.380
the norms around that in code.

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:47.426
The second is preregistration
and ways of thinking about it.

00:03:47.426 --> 00:03:49.300
And then I'm going to
try to get to the third

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:52.600
very quickly, because I
think it falls after Brian's

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:55.570
talk in terms of
thinking about what's

00:03:55.570 --> 00:03:58.120
the future of
scientific conversation

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:00.700
that a platform like OSF
or others could build

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:03.070
and could enable us.

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:09.850
OK, so we want scientists
to share all relevant files

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:13.000
for understanding where their
knowledge claims come from.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:14.470
That is practical.

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:18.040
Ultimately, I was thinking
when Brian was talking, what

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:19.980
is the utopia of transparency?

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:23.560
Is it like a 1984 surveillance
state, where we have cameras

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:25.390
on, and everything gets shared?

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:28.690
And I know one colleague of mine
who really wanted to live up

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:33.550
to transparency norms posted
in their replication archive

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:38.350
all the emails between them
and their coders, their coding

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:39.010
history.

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:42.430
So there was
thousands of emails.

00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:45.130
And so any critical
reader can go and find out

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:46.960
exactly why was this
historical observation

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:48.710
coded in this way
and not another way.

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:53.830
So that might be the limit.

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:55.654
The transparency
maxim I'll offer

00:04:55.654 --> 00:04:57.820
is that we should try to
make every step of research

00:04:57.820 --> 00:05:01.030
as explicit and reproducible
as is practical.

00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:02.740
Clearly, at some
point, we're going

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:05.030
to hit diminishing returns
or other constraints.

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:07.270
So we have to leave it
up to each discipline

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:10.370
and each researcher
to negotiate that.

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:12.620
But the benefits are tremendous.

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:15.070
And Brian made most
of these points.

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:17.350
One I want to
emphasize is the idea

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:20.140
that scientific
publications used

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:24.970
to be like a scrapbook
of photos, of someone who

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:28.550
went on a vacation or travel.

00:05:28.550 --> 00:05:30.880
And they are showing us the
pictures of their journey.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:32.680
And they're telling
us a story from that.

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:34.510
And that narrative
is only as good

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:36.460
as we trust the
author's representation

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:37.900
of what they saw.

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:40.240
And there, the
analogy is to data.

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:42.640
Whereas with the internet,
with modern computing,

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:45.370
we no longer need to have
the static representation

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:46.240
of the data.

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:49.710
Now, I think of the publication
like an advertisement,

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:51.110
as like a guided tour.

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:53.352
So the authors say,
come with me, reader.

00:05:53.352 --> 00:05:55.060
I will walk you through
a data landscape,

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:57.310
and show you what I found,
and show you what's there.

00:05:57.310 --> 00:06:00.550
But the reader should be free
to leave the tour at any moment.

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:02.800
They can say, I'm curious
about what's over that hill,

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:04.972
or what's behind that curtain.

00:06:04.972 --> 00:06:06.430
And they should be
able to do that.

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:10.570
So I think that's
where we're going just

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:15.550
in terms of facilitating the
reproduction of analysis.

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:19.590
OK, just a fun figure to
share-- so I collected data.

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:22.330
And these are the two leading
political science journals.

00:06:22.330 --> 00:06:25.800
And the y-axis is the
proportion that state,

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:29.130
or whether the article states
that replication files are

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:30.420
available.

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:35.100
So a few takeaways-- one, in
the time period 2009 to 2013,

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:37.710
at least if we look at APSR on
the right, which is our number

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:40.110
one journal, it wasn't great.

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:42.750
AJPS, on the other hand,
started out around 0,

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:44.430
and then jumps to about 100%.

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:47.160
And as you can
probably infer, that's

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:49.350
because a new
editor came on board

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:53.700
and added a policy that says--
all published AJPS articles,

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:56.100
the first footnote must
state where replication files

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:57.060
are available.

00:06:57.060 --> 00:07:00.240
So a small commitment
like that by a journal

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:03.560
generated 100% compliance.

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:05.680
View the letter of the
law, but not the spirit.

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:08.980
So now the y-axis is-- are
replication files actually

00:07:08.980 --> 00:07:09.820
available?

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:14.950
And nevertheless, we still have
50% to 25% of articles in AJPS,

00:07:14.950 --> 00:07:17.950
despite a footnote saying "our
replication files are available

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:21.130
here," no replication
files can be found there,

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:23.080
or given about 10
minutes of searching,

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:25.620
anywhere else on the internet.

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:27.810
Hopefully, I think the
community is adapting.

00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:29.330
We see a creep up.

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:32.160
And I should say, political
science has really

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:35.790
embraced transparency
norms and standards,

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:40.020
in part because of the
TOP movement transparency

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:43.240
and openness
promotion guidelines.

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:44.630
So we're getting better.

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:47.510
But this is just to
show that it really

00:07:47.510 --> 00:07:52.014
helps to have journals
providing incentives.

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:59.780
OK, so what do we want in
complete replication files?

00:07:59.780 --> 00:08:02.630
We might want even the
e-mails between the PI

00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:05.930
in the lab members.

00:08:05.930 --> 00:08:07.190
But I won't go that far.

00:08:07.190 --> 00:08:11.780
I do think we want as far
down in the chain of inference

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:12.374
as possible.

00:08:12.374 --> 00:08:13.790
And you can think
of it as we want

00:08:13.790 --> 00:08:17.180
to maintain a chain of custody
of how the original data led

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:20.360
to this final claim.

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:21.860
And then one other
point I just want

00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:25.790
to share that I
think is symptomatic

00:08:25.790 --> 00:08:30.590
of the state of, at least, maybe
political science, but maybe

00:08:30.590 --> 00:08:33.380
other social sciences, is that
I work with surveys and survey

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:34.340
experiments.

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:37.640
And the key thing you want
when you're reading a survey

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:41.210
is to see the original
survey text verbatim,

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:45.240
how it's laid out, the question
that preceded the key question,

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:48.972
because text can
put the respondent

00:08:48.972 --> 00:08:50.180
in a different frame of mind.

00:08:50.180 --> 00:08:52.430
They interpret later
questions differently.

00:08:52.430 --> 00:08:55.160
So we know that
this matters a lot.

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:57.590
And yet, to my amazement,
leading scholars

00:08:57.590 --> 00:09:00.250
in my profession
and my discipline

00:09:00.250 --> 00:09:04.640
will publish surveys and
survey experiments without.

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:08.810
Nowhere on the internet is the
actual survey text available.

00:09:08.810 --> 00:09:12.440
And so this is not due to
technical difficulties.

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:14.690
It is not hard to take a
picture, or a screenshot,

00:09:14.690 --> 00:09:18.530
or post a PDF of your survey,
or a QSL file, for example,

00:09:18.530 --> 00:09:19.790
if you use Qualtrics.

00:09:19.790 --> 00:09:22.940
So I think what this reflects
is either a lack of appreciation

00:09:22.940 --> 00:09:26.720
of the importance of this
level of transparency,

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:29.741
or people think they can get
away with it-- some combination

00:09:29.741 --> 00:09:30.240
of the two.

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:32.990
So Brian is great.

00:09:32.990 --> 00:09:37.880
He builds us tools to
advance transparency.

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:41.900
I will be a little bit more
of the cynic or realist.

00:09:41.900 --> 00:09:44.330
I think we also need the stick.

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:44.900
It's here.

00:09:44.900 --> 00:09:46.870
We don't need a tool
to get this happening.

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:49.370
We just need colleagues
to know it is not

00:09:49.370 --> 00:09:53.150
acceptable to produce a survey
or survey experimental study

00:09:53.150 --> 00:09:58.350
and not show the PDF publicly,
or the text of the survey.

00:09:58.350 --> 00:10:01.790
OK, and we could even
make stronger norms.

00:10:01.790 --> 00:10:04.460
We could have journals
think that the paper should

00:10:04.460 --> 00:10:06.510
be retracted if it
can't be reproduced

00:10:06.510 --> 00:10:08.835
from the original data.

00:10:08.835 --> 00:10:10.710
And then, of course,
also as Brian mentioned,

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:13.490
we should find ways of
weighting transparency

00:10:13.490 --> 00:10:15.350
in the incentive structure
where it matters.

00:10:15.350 --> 00:10:18.830
We could rank and
judge journals.

00:10:18.830 --> 00:10:22.280
And then when considering
hiring and promoting scholars,

00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:27.600
we should upweight
transparency as a criterion.

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:30.940
OK, so this is the beautiful
figure about the 100 psychology

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:32.560
studies.

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:36.010
And the main takeaway
here is some replicated.

00:10:36.010 --> 00:10:38.950
The replicated effect
was about the same

00:10:38.950 --> 00:10:40.500
as the original effect.

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:43.060
But some many did not replicate.

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:45.160
They were the estimated
effect, and replication

00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:47.440
was around zero.

00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:50.710
And this issue is not
addressed by what I've just

00:10:50.710 --> 00:10:53.620
been discussing, the
sharing of code and data,

00:10:53.620 --> 00:10:55.769
because this could be
due to publication bias.

00:10:55.769 --> 00:10:57.310
It could be due to
the researcher who

00:10:57.310 --> 00:11:00.460
runs 100 experiments
and shares the five that

00:11:00.460 --> 00:11:02.560
are most interesting.

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:04.800
So to address that, we
need preregistration.

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:06.790
Or there are some
other solutions.

00:11:06.790 --> 00:11:09.490
And I just want to flag a
trade off in preregistration.

00:11:09.490 --> 00:11:12.700
That unlike data sharing and
code sharing, which I think

00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:17.470
is unarguable-- there's
very little cost to it.

00:11:17.470 --> 00:11:20.260
With preregistration,
in my own experience,

00:11:20.260 --> 00:11:24.280
and especially with
my students, there's

00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:27.520
a risk of spending too much time
at the pre-registration stage,

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:29.850
and really slowing down
the research process.

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:35.424
So students will, because I'll
give a very emphatic lecture

00:11:35.424 --> 00:11:37.090
about the importance
of preregistration,

00:11:37.090 --> 00:11:41.860
they will spend a month or
two theorizing and designing

00:11:41.860 --> 00:11:43.270
their study.

00:11:43.270 --> 00:11:45.130
And then when they do
it, they get a null.

00:11:45.130 --> 00:11:49.780
So that's a month or
two potentially wasted.

00:11:49.780 --> 00:11:52.030
Hopefully, there's benefits.

00:11:52.030 --> 00:11:53.770
But the main point
that I want to make

00:11:53.770 --> 00:11:57.080
is that a lot of science is
exploratory and inductive.

00:11:57.080 --> 00:11:59.350
And a lot of the value
that's generated from science

00:11:59.350 --> 00:12:01.840
comes from the exploratory
inductive process.

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:05.500
So we just need to find ways
of preserving that value

00:12:05.500 --> 00:12:07.240
and recognizing
that it's valuable,

00:12:07.240 --> 00:12:11.890
at the same time as insisting
that work distinguishes

00:12:11.890 --> 00:12:14.410
whether it's confirmatory or
exploratory by systematically

00:12:14.410 --> 00:12:16.690
doing preregistration.

00:12:16.690 --> 00:12:19.330
And then the other pitfall is
that anticipating this time

00:12:19.330 --> 00:12:21.970
cost, scholars don't
pre-register at all.

00:12:21.970 --> 00:12:27.000
So my recommendation is
pre-register all the time,

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:30.690
but don't require yourself to
have a perfect preregistration.

00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:34.590
Don't require it be a fully
fleshed theory necessarily,

00:12:34.590 --> 00:12:37.770
if you're not there, with
full pre-analysis code.

00:12:37.770 --> 00:12:40.050
That's great if you can do
it, but if it's just one

00:12:40.050 --> 00:12:41.700
paragraph, that's great.

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:44.850
And OSF, as I understand
as Brian's been using it,

00:12:44.850 --> 00:12:47.850
has enabled that different
levels of sharing.

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:51.060
So you might have a summary
minutes from your lab meeting,

00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:53.110
and that would be
your preregistration.

00:12:53.110 --> 00:12:56.510
I think that's a
good compromise.

00:12:56.510 --> 00:12:57.660
And there's other ways.

00:12:57.660 --> 00:13:00.210
[INAUDIBLE] mentions,
I think, a good way,

00:13:00.210 --> 00:13:02.310
where you take up the
treatment variable,

00:13:02.310 --> 00:13:04.090
and then you analyze your data.

00:13:04.090 --> 00:13:06.090
So then you get to see
what the data looks like,

00:13:06.090 --> 00:13:09.560
and how the outcomes correlates
with the various covariates.

00:13:09.560 --> 00:13:13.320
But you can't p-hack, because
the treatment variable is not

00:13:13.320 --> 00:13:14.670
connected to the outcome.

00:13:14.670 --> 00:13:17.090
So that's a promising way.

00:13:17.090 --> 00:13:18.560
OK, and I might
come back to that.

00:13:23.590 --> 00:13:25.390
OK, yeah, so I want
to get to this.

00:13:25.390 --> 00:13:28.690
So this speaks to
Brian's later slides,

00:13:28.690 --> 00:13:32.500
which are what's the future
of scientific conversation?

00:13:32.500 --> 00:13:36.550
And this idea that publication
and evaluation are currently

00:13:36.550 --> 00:13:40.210
are intertwined and should be
separated is exactly right.

00:13:40.210 --> 00:13:42.220
And I'll just sketch
a vision that I

00:13:42.220 --> 00:13:48.430
think OSF is moving
towards, and other groups

00:13:48.430 --> 00:13:51.682
are starting to build out
the features for this.

00:13:51.682 --> 00:13:53.140
And I'll tell you
the vision that I

00:13:53.140 --> 00:13:54.997
think would be
excellent, and I think

00:13:54.997 --> 00:13:56.830
is an attractor for
scientific conversation.

00:13:56.830 --> 00:13:59.320
And given how many
scientists we have here,

00:13:59.320 --> 00:14:02.374
I would love to know your
thoughts if this makes sense.

00:14:02.374 --> 00:14:03.790
So if I want to
buy a book, here's

00:14:03.790 --> 00:14:08.110
Gelman and Hill's data
analysis textbook.

00:14:08.110 --> 00:14:11.560
I can go to the Cambridge
publisher's website.

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:12.380
And what do I get?

00:14:12.380 --> 00:14:13.030
I get a title.

00:14:13.030 --> 00:14:14.029
I get their author name.

00:14:14.029 --> 00:14:16.270
I get the price, the cover art.

00:14:16.270 --> 00:14:17.770
I can look inside,
so I can actually

00:14:17.770 --> 00:14:20.560
read the whole, or
much of the book.

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:23.302
But what I don't get
is what I start off

00:14:23.302 --> 00:14:24.760
by saying we need
in science, which

00:14:24.760 --> 00:14:28.810
is the sort of synthesized
expert evaluation of my peers

00:14:28.810 --> 00:14:29.632
and colleagues.

00:14:29.632 --> 00:14:31.840
I don't want to have to read
the whole book to decide

00:14:31.840 --> 00:14:33.100
whether I should buy it.

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:36.370
I want to delegate to my
colleagues, who have already

00:14:36.370 --> 00:14:38.500
read it, their judgment.

00:14:38.500 --> 00:14:42.520
And then integrate it
in some appropriate way.

00:14:42.520 --> 00:14:44.110
We now have that service.

00:14:44.110 --> 00:14:45.970
Amazon provides it for us.

00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:48.386
These aren't necessarily my
colleagues who are doing this,

00:14:48.386 --> 00:14:49.720
though sometimes they are.

00:14:49.720 --> 00:14:52.120
But you have a
community of readers

00:14:52.120 --> 00:14:56.340
who volunteer time to
share their judgment

00:14:56.340 --> 00:14:58.525
and assessment of a book.

00:14:58.525 --> 00:15:00.400
Not only do you have
the quantitative ratings

00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:02.650
which is a useful
way to screen books,

00:15:02.650 --> 00:15:06.280
but you also have a detailed
review and a holistic review.

00:15:06.280 --> 00:15:08.710
Amazon also helpfully
gives us this

00:15:08.710 --> 00:15:11.710
what did these customers
who bought this also buy,

00:15:11.710 --> 00:15:15.460
which is a useful
service that currently--

00:15:15.460 --> 00:15:19.780
well, at least the Cambridge
website would not provide.

00:15:19.780 --> 00:15:23.830
So here is an article
from a leading journal.

00:15:23.830 --> 00:15:25.960
And again, we get what
the publisher provides.

00:15:25.960 --> 00:15:28.510
So we get the details,
the title, the abstract,

00:15:28.510 --> 00:15:30.680
the author name.

00:15:30.680 --> 00:15:32.650
But it's all shared.

00:15:32.650 --> 00:15:35.980
It's all provided by a
self-interested group.

00:15:35.980 --> 00:15:39.029
The authors have motivation
to portray the results

00:15:39.029 --> 00:15:39.820
in a certain light.

00:15:39.820 --> 00:15:42.740
The publishers have a motivation
to portray it in certain light.

00:15:42.740 --> 00:15:45.280
There's no third
party here telling me

00:15:45.280 --> 00:15:47.531
what they thought of this.

00:15:47.531 --> 00:15:49.280
And so that's where I
think we want to go.

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:54.650
We want to build a web
platform or an online forum

00:15:54.650 --> 00:15:58.542
where the community discusses
works, hopefully expresses them

00:15:58.542 --> 00:16:00.500
in a quantitative metric
that can be aggregated

00:16:00.500 --> 00:16:06.724
in a sensible way, to facilitate
my assessment of other works.

00:16:06.724 --> 00:16:09.140
So that I don't have to spend
two hours reading this paper

00:16:09.140 --> 00:16:11.810
to figure out if I trust
the results or not.

00:16:11.810 --> 00:16:13.550
My colleague, who
already did that,

00:16:13.550 --> 00:16:14.990
can basically tell
me indirectly.

00:16:14.990 --> 00:16:18.920
So we have this more
efficient conversation.

00:16:18.920 --> 00:16:21.370
This already happens offline.

00:16:21.370 --> 00:16:23.900
If I want to know if
this work is any good,

00:16:23.900 --> 00:16:26.750
I might email my colleague
who I know has read it,

00:16:26.750 --> 00:16:28.490
and ask them for their input.

00:16:28.490 --> 00:16:31.070
So we just want to build a
web platform so that everyone

00:16:31.070 --> 00:16:35.930
has access to that, and it
facilitates conversation.

00:16:35.930 --> 00:16:39.475
So here's one group
that at least provided

00:16:39.475 --> 00:16:40.850
a visual representation
of what I

00:16:40.850 --> 00:16:42.099
think it could look like this.

00:16:42.099 --> 00:16:43.470
Is Science Open.

00:16:43.470 --> 00:16:46.890
Here you have an article,
the same title, authors,

00:16:46.890 --> 00:16:48.480
maybe an abstract.

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:50.130
And then what I'm
really looking for

00:16:50.130 --> 00:16:52.590
is the quantitative
summary of what

00:16:52.590 --> 00:16:54.610
readers think of this work.

00:16:54.610 --> 00:16:56.500
And then you can imagine
a holistic review

00:16:56.500 --> 00:16:59.680
down here that's been up
voted by all the readers.

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:02.380
So the first thing
you would see is

00:17:02.380 --> 00:17:05.270
an expert judgment,
third party, saying,

00:17:05.270 --> 00:17:06.760
this is what I
think of this piece.

00:17:06.760 --> 00:17:11.619
And it's not just a troll
or a negative response.

00:17:11.619 --> 00:17:12.942
It's someone who really shares.

00:17:12.942 --> 00:17:14.650
This is what this work
achieves, and this

00:17:14.650 --> 00:17:15.650
is where it falls short.

00:17:15.650 --> 00:17:16.986
These are the scope conditions.

00:17:16.986 --> 00:17:18.069
This is what's impressive.

00:17:18.069 --> 00:17:20.900
This is what you can look out
for when you're reading this.

00:17:20.900 --> 00:17:24.010
A third party perspective that
I can quickly get to that's

00:17:24.010 --> 00:17:27.880
already been crowdsourced
by the community,

00:17:27.880 --> 00:17:33.680
so that when I get there,
it's at the top of visibility.

00:17:33.680 --> 00:17:35.890
OK, there's a lot of things
we could add to that.

00:17:35.890 --> 00:17:37.720
And I'll just list some.

00:17:37.720 --> 00:17:40.515
So there's this idea
of a wiki abstract.

00:17:40.515 --> 00:17:41.890
Rather than have
the authors who,

00:17:41.890 --> 00:17:43.723
again, have this
self-interest in presenting

00:17:43.723 --> 00:17:47.890
the results as being
innovative and outstanding,

00:17:47.890 --> 00:17:50.830
I would love to share with
what expert reviewers think was

00:17:50.830 --> 00:17:52.780
actually achieved in the paper.

00:17:52.780 --> 00:17:54.790
What did the results
actually show?

00:17:54.790 --> 00:17:57.674
So more disinterested
representation--

00:17:57.674 --> 00:17:59.090
the ratings could
be unimportance,

00:17:59.090 --> 00:18:00.220
credibility, clarity.

00:18:00.220 --> 00:18:02.428
Those are three metrics that
I think would be useful.

00:18:05.390 --> 00:18:08.849
You probably don't want
just some naive averaging

00:18:08.849 --> 00:18:10.640
over everyone's ratings,
especially if it's

00:18:10.640 --> 00:18:12.770
really open to the public.

00:18:12.770 --> 00:18:15.150
You wouldn't want to
read climate science

00:18:15.150 --> 00:18:18.680
quantitative ratings if a
certain political group decides

00:18:18.680 --> 00:18:21.890
to get on there and
down vote all of them.

00:18:21.890 --> 00:18:24.290
Or even maybe one
lab systematically

00:18:24.290 --> 00:18:27.380
down votes another lab's work.

00:18:27.380 --> 00:18:30.620
So you can have all kinds
of customized ratings

00:18:30.620 --> 00:18:33.350
where you might
subset on scholars

00:18:33.350 --> 00:18:40.055
of a certain achievement,
or a certain social network.

00:18:40.055 --> 00:18:41.930
Entrepreneurs could even
provide the service.

00:18:41.930 --> 00:18:45.980
They could say, use
my ratings algorithm,

00:18:45.980 --> 00:18:51.440
which pulls from esteemed
respected scholars.

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:54.656
OK, so then the community would
up vote useful contributions,

00:18:54.656 --> 00:18:56.030
so that the first
thing you'd see

00:18:56.030 --> 00:18:58.490
like in Stack Exchange,
or Reddit, or Quora,

00:18:58.490 --> 00:19:02.300
or Amazon reviews is the
overall most helpful assessment

00:19:02.300 --> 00:19:03.860
of the piece.

00:19:03.860 --> 00:19:07.130
But also contributors who
are systematically helpful

00:19:07.130 --> 00:19:10.460
would be up voted, and then
every subsequent contribution

00:19:10.460 --> 00:19:12.770
that they make would be
more likely to be visible.

00:19:12.770 --> 00:19:15.740
And conversely, contributors
who are systematically

00:19:15.740 --> 00:19:18.830
writing ad hominem things
or unhelpful comments,

00:19:18.830 --> 00:19:20.960
would be down voted, and
would have difficulties

00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:23.630
getting visibility.

00:19:23.630 --> 00:19:27.025
You can have a
range of anonymity.

00:19:27.025 --> 00:19:28.400
Though I think
you would probably

00:19:28.400 --> 00:19:32.750
want each user to
have some connection

00:19:32.750 --> 00:19:36.080
to an actual human
identity, though you could

00:19:36.080 --> 00:19:38.507
try with an anonymous model.

00:19:38.507 --> 00:19:41.090
But you could go all the way to
a signed model, where everyone

00:19:41.090 --> 00:19:43.702
has to use their real name.

00:19:43.702 --> 00:19:46.160
Though I suspect, given the
prevalence of single blind peer

00:19:46.160 --> 00:19:47.990
review at least in
science, we probably

00:19:47.990 --> 00:19:51.020
want to preserve at least
some kind of blinded mode.

00:19:51.020 --> 00:19:54.440
So you could log in,
make your contributions

00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:57.222
under a pseudonym,
but the editors

00:19:57.222 --> 00:19:58.430
would still know who you are.

00:19:58.430 --> 00:20:00.740
And so that would
prevent collusion

00:20:00.740 --> 00:20:04.280
amongst circles of scholars who
are up voting or down voting

00:20:04.280 --> 00:20:05.480
their rivals.

00:20:05.480 --> 00:20:06.260
It would prevent.

00:20:06.260 --> 00:20:08.720
It would help you
identify trolls.

00:20:08.720 --> 00:20:11.900
And I think it would just help
keep the whole process more

00:20:11.900 --> 00:20:12.950
productive.

00:20:12.950 --> 00:20:15.710
And then last, a fun
idea to throw out there,

00:20:15.710 --> 00:20:23.120
for any metric that is
socially exogenous and really

00:20:23.120 --> 00:20:25.570
verifiable, is
either zero or one.

00:20:25.570 --> 00:20:27.710
You could build a
prediction market onto it.

00:20:27.710 --> 00:20:31.340
So the obvious application
is replicability.

00:20:31.340 --> 00:20:33.254
So if there is an
experiment that's done,

00:20:33.254 --> 00:20:34.670
you could have a
prediction market

00:20:34.670 --> 00:20:36.420
for whether the
experiment would replicate

00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:40.690
according to some
standard of replication.

00:20:40.690 --> 00:20:45.640
And there's various ways, but
if the replication is ever

00:20:45.640 --> 00:20:47.500
done according to
some standard, perhaps

00:20:47.500 --> 00:20:50.860
by some consortium of labs,
then the prediction market

00:20:50.860 --> 00:20:51.970
would pay out.

00:20:51.970 --> 00:20:54.969
So I think it's
inevitable that science

00:20:54.969 --> 00:20:56.260
will go to something like this.

00:20:56.260 --> 00:20:59.700
There will be a much richer
conversation in 5 and 10 years

00:20:59.700 --> 00:21:00.730
online.

00:21:00.730 --> 00:21:04.490
I can imagine it matters
how we get there.

00:21:04.490 --> 00:21:06.970
Or I can imagine there's path
dependence if we get there.

00:21:06.970 --> 00:21:09.040
If the scientists
own the platform,

00:21:09.040 --> 00:21:12.250
it could be more productive
than if a private company owns

00:21:12.250 --> 00:21:16.007
the platform, and the culture
that we establish going in.

00:21:16.007 --> 00:21:17.590
So yeah, I look
forward your thoughts.

00:21:17.590 --> 00:21:18.790
And if anyone is really
interested in this,

00:21:18.790 --> 00:21:20.560
I'd love to talk
with you afterwards.

00:21:20.560 --> 00:21:22.060
Thanks.

00:21:22.060 --> 00:21:25.110
[APPLAUSE]

